========================================================================
TF CHAIN — cost of publishing lidar_tilt_joint faster
========================================================================
  Private namespace /bench: its own joint_state_publisher +
  robot_state_publisher.  The real /lidar_tilt/joint_states is NOT
  touched — a second publisher there would fight server_x3.py for
  the /scan safety gate.

  bench nodes: robot_state_pub(4113), joint_state_pub(4114)   (jsp rate param = 10 Hz)

  cmd Hz          js Hz      tf Hz  jsp %core  rsp %core
  10               10.0        8.9        4.4        1.4
  25               25.0       10.0        6.7        1.0
  50               50.0       10.0       10.9        1.2
  100             100.0       10.0       16.9        0.9

  (jsp/rsp %core are on top of each other; the real stack also
   carries wheel joints, so treat these as a lower bound.)

========================================================================
OCTOMAP — the load that actually scales with sweep rate
========================================================================
  Private /bench octomap_server fed REAL /scan_raw geometry,
  re-pitched through the sweep range so the raycasts are as long
  and as varied as a real sweep.  The live octomap is untouched.

  captured 12 real scans (2793 beams each)
  bank: 12 clouds, 2638 points mean, pitched -45..+45 deg
  raycast work per cloud ~ 2638 pts x 160 voxels = 0.42 M traversals
  bench octomap pid [4269]

  pub Hz           sent   inserted   keep up?  oct %core
  2.5               2.3        2.3       100%       23.1
  7.2               6.7        6.7       100%       58.5
  14.4             13.5       12.4        92%       97.5

  "keep up?" below ~95%% means octomap_server is dropping clouds: it is
  single-threaded, so the message filter backs up and you get the
  "queue is full" symptom rather than an error.

